  "zipball_url": "https://api.github.com/repos/unkn0wn-root/resterm/zipball/v1.18.1",
  "body": "# v1.18.1\r\n\r\nThis patch fixes gzip responses for requests that set their own `Accept-Encoding` header, and makes secret masking a bit faster.\r\n\r\n## Gzip with your own `Accept-Encoding`\r\n\r\nResterm only decoded gzip when it added `Accept-Encoding` itself. If the request set that header, the body stayed compressed, so `@assert`, captures, scripts and the response pane all got gzip bytes. Requests imported with `curl --compressed` set the header too.\r\n\r\n```http\r\n### Users\r\n# @name users\r\n# @assert response.json()[0].name == \"david\"\r\nGET http://localhost:3000/users\r\nAccept-Encoding: gzip\r\n```\r\n\r\nBefore 1.18.1, the check failed:\r\n\r\n```text\r\nFAIL GET users [users.http:3:24: invalid json]\r\n  Script Error:\r\n    error[script]: invalid json\r\n    --> users.http:3:24\r\n         |\r\n       3 | # @assert response.json()[0].name == \"david\"\r\n         |                        ^\r\n```\r\n\r\nNow it passes:\r\n\r\n```text\r\nPASS GET users [200 OK in 728.875µs]\r\n```\r\n\r\n- A gzip response is decoded whether Resterm or the request asked for it. The decoded response has no `Content-Encoding` or `Content-Length` header, the same as when Resterm asks for gzip itself.\r\n- Gzip that can't be decoded used to pass with the broken body. Now the request fails with exit 26:\r\n\r\n```text\r\nFAIL GET bad [read response body: invalid gzip header: gzip: invalid header]\r\n```\r\n\r\n- SSE streams are decoded as they arrive, so events, `byteCount` and `max-bytes` use the decoded data, not the compressed bytes. An event stream in an encoding Resterm can't decode, such as `br`, now fails with exit 26 instead of passing.\r\n- `br`, `deflate` and `206 Partial Content` bodies stay as they arrived.\r\n- `curl --compressed` imports now write `Accept-Encoding: gzip` instead of `gzip, deflate, br`, since Resterm can't decode the other two.\r\n- With `--response`, `bodySize` is the decoded size.\r\n\r\nSee [HTTP settings](https://resterm.app/docs/http-settings/) and [streaming](https://resterm.app/docs/streaming/).\r\n\r\n## Faster masking\r\n\r\nMasking secrets in JSON is faster. A 1 MiB JSON body now takes about 6 ms instead of 11 ms. This covers `--response` bodies, history, stream transcripts and trace files.\r\n\r\n## Upgrading\r\n\r\n1. Decoded gzip responses have no `Content-Encoding` or `Content-Length` header. Update checks that read them.\r\n2. Gzip that can't be decoded fails the request with exit 26. So does an event stream in another encoding, such as `br`.\r\n3. Requests imported with `curl --compressed` before 1.18.1 still ask for `gzip, deflate, br`. A `br` or `deflate` body is kept as it arrived, so change the header to `gzip`.\r\n\r\nIf you're upgrading from 1.17.2 or older, read the [1.18.0 notes](https://github.com/unkn0wn-root/resterm/releases/tag/v1.18.0).\r\n"
